.TH WMAKERMSG 1 "2026" "Window Maker"
.SH NAME
wmakermsg \- send commands to and receive events from Window Maker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmakermsg
[\fB\-s\fR \fISOCKET\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] \fIVERB\fR [\fIARG\fR...]
.br
.B wmakermsg
[\fB\-s\fR \fISOCKET\fR] \fBget_version\fR|\fBget_workspaces\fR|\fBget_windows\fR|\fBget_window_tree\fR
.br
.B wmakermsg
[\fB\-s\fR \fISOCKET\fR] \fBsend_tick\fR \fIPAYLOAD\fR
.br
.B wmakermsg
[\fB\-s\fR \fISOCKET\fR] \fB\-m\fR \fIEVENT\fR [\fIEVENT\fR...]
.br
.B wmakermsg
\fB\-\-get\-socketpath\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wmakermsg
talks to a running Window Maker instance over its Unix-socket control
channel. The socket is located via the
.B WMAKER_IPC_SOCKET
environment variable when set, otherwise derived from
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
and
.BR DISPLAY .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-get\-socketpath
Print the resolved socket path for the current
.B DISPLAY
and exit without connecting.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the reply as JSON instead of property-list text. A reply that
does not parse as a property list is printed to stderr and
.B wmakermsg
exits 1.
.I get_window_tree
implies this option.
.TP
.B \-m
Monitor mode. Subscribe to the named event types and print one event per
line until the connection closes or the process is terminated. Output is
flushed after every event so it can be consumed with
.BR "while read -r" .
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress the reply body, exit status alone reflects success.
.TP
.BI \-s " SOCKET"
Connect to the given socket path instead of resolving one.
.SH COMMAND VERBS
.BR reconfigure ,
.BR "restart [WM]" ,
.BR exit ,
.BR kill\-session ,
.BR "workspace next|prev|last|N" ,
.BR "window\-workspace XID N|+N|\-N" ,
.BR "set\-layout LAYOUT [workspace N] [master_count N] [master_ratio F]" ,
.BR "set\-viewport X Y" ,
.BR "dock\-launch SLOT" ,
.BR "workspace\-new [count]" ,
.BR "workspace\-delete N" ,
.BR "display\-power on|off" ,
.BR "play\-sound EVENT" ,
.BR "action \fINAME\fR [\fIXID\fR]" ,
.BR info ,
.BR "get\-keybinds [all]" .
.PP
The
.B get\-keybinds
verb lists the active key bindings as a
.I keybinds
array. Each row reports the short
.IR action
name, the configuration key, the raw configuration spec, a category and a
human-readable description, and whether it is bound, plus the rendered
display label for bound rows. By default unbound rows are omitted; pass
.I all
to include them. Use
.B \-\-json
to consume the reply as JSON.
.PP
The
.B action
verb runs a key\-binding action on the focused window, or on
.I XID
when given.
.I NAME
is either the short action name (for example
.IR NextWorkspace )
or its configuration key, as reported in the
.I actions
array of the
.B info
reply. An unknown action name is rejected with
.IR "unknown action" .
.PP
The
.B display\-power
verb tells the window manager that no output is showing the
framebuffer. While
.I off
all decorative animations are skipped and the urgent\-icon bounce and
selection\-ants timers disarm so an idle session reaches zero wakeups.
Intended to be driven from a lid\-switch or screensaver hook, the
window manager also flips this state itself on RandR output loss and
on MIT\-SCREEN\-SAVER notify when those extensions are available.
.SH TYPED REQUESTS
The
.IR get_version ,
.IR get_workspaces ,
.I get_windows
requests return their respective snapshots as property-list text.
The
.I get_window_tree
request returns the whole window-manager state (applications with
their windows, tab groups, stacking, dock attachment, geometry and
state flags) as JSON, and accepts no arguments.
The
.I send_tick
request broadcasts a tick event carrying its payload argument.
.SH EVENT TYPES
.BR workspace ,
.BR window ,
.BR shutdown ,
.BR child_exit ,
.BR dialog ,
.BR tick ,
.BR screenshot ,
.BR display_power .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
success,
.B 1
transport failure (could not connect, read, write, or protocol error),
.B 2
the server replied
.I success = NO
to a command verb.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wmaker (1),
.BR wdread (1),
.B doc/wmaker-ipc.txt
in the source distribution.
